link/connection. The 5 edge weight labels (80%, 70%, 90%, 60%, 50%) stayed fixed through every interaction, including competency-weight changes. Only per-competency weight sliders exist, which are a different quantity.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-5\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Disabled state is not shown on the node/edges, and a disabled competency cannot be re-enabled (repeated toggles do not act on current state)\", \"actual\": \"Disabling \\\"Programming Languages\\\" correctly excluded it from analysis (coverage 66%→65%, avg 3.3→3.2, gap alert cleared), but the UI showed no disabled state: the toggle kept data-state=\\\"checked\\\", the node wrapper kept opacity 1 and unchanged classes (competency-node ... border-node-critical), and all 5 edges kept opacity 1 with unchanged stroke/dash. Clicking the same toggle a second time to re-enable had no effect — coverage stayed 65%, avg 3.2, gaps 0, toggle still \\\"checked\\\" — so the competency remained disabled and could not be restored.\", \"evidence\": \"Exclusion math verified: enabled weights .8,.6,.9,.7,.5 → coverage 11.4/5/3.5=65%, avg (3+3+4+3+3)/5=3.2.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-6\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Template library lists 5 job-role templates, each showing role, department, competency count, relationship count and representative keywords: Software Engineer/Engineering/6 competencies/5 connections/Programming,Coding,Software,Development; Product Manager/Product/6/5/Product,Management,Strategy,Roadmap; HR Manager/Human Resources/6/5/Hr,Human Resources,Recruitment,Talent; Data Scientist/Analytics/6/5/Data,Machine Learning,Analytics,Statistics; Sales Manager/Sales/6/5/Sales,Revenue,Business Development,Customer. Current model marked \\\"Current\\\" with its Apply disabled.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-7\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Applying a template does not replace the competency graph; the graph keeps showing the previous model's competencies and relationships\", \"actual\": \"Applying the \\\"HR Manager\\\" template updated the title to \\\"HR Manager\\\", department \\\"Human Resources\\\", metrics (coverage 71%, avg 3.5, critical 3), the \\\"Current\\\" badge, and the scenario category adjustments (Technical 0 / Behavioral 1 / Leadership 2 / Domain 3). However the graph still rendered the Software Engineer nodes — Programming Languages, System Design, Code Review, Problem Solving, Collaboration, Agile Methodology (node ids prog-lang, sys-design, code-review, problem-solve, collab, agile) — with the unchanged edge weights 80%, 70%, 90%, 60%, 50%. Underlying state confirms the model is HR Manager with competencies talent-acq, emp-relations, hr-compliance, leadership, perf-mgmt, org-dev, so the graph contradicts the applied template.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-8\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Saving the current model as a custom template is not implemented; no name/description prompt and no My Templates collection\", \"actual\": \"After editing the model (Collaboration → level 5, coverage 75%), clicking \\\"Save Current\\\" (templates.save-current) produced no visible response: no dialog or alertdialog element, no name/description inputs, no toast/status message, and no new semantic elements. The template list still contained only the 5 built-in role templates (Software Engineer, Product Manager, HR Manager, Data Scientist, Sales Manager). The text \\\"My Templates\\\" does not appear anywhere in the page, and localStorage remained completely empty, so there is no custom template to retrieve after leaving and returning.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-9\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Switching tabs updated both the active scenario heading/description and the category adjustments: Recruitment = \\\"Simulate candidate profiles and hiring decisions\\\" (Behavioral +10% weight, Leadership -2 level/-10% weight, Domain -1 level); Training = \\\"Model the impact of training programs\\\" (Domain +1 level); Promotion = \\\"Assess readiness for role advancement\\\" (Behavioral +10% weight, Leadership +20% weight). Each switch re-rendered simulation.active-scenario accordingly.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-10\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Category-level adjustments are selectable via per-category Level Change and Weight Change sliders (dragged Leadership level -2→+2, Technical level→+2 and weight→+25%, displays updated). Running the simulation applied them per category: leadership competencies L3→5, domain L4→3/L3→2, behavioral weight 0.85→0.95, leadership weights 0.75→0.65 and 0.70→0.60, with outputs updating (avg level 3.5→3.7, risk gaps 0→1; later run coverage 68%→87%). Reset restored the model to its original state: Software Engineer with prog-lang L4 W0.9, sys-design L3 W0.8, problem-solve L4 W0.9, collab L3 W0.7, code-review L3 W0.6, agile L3 W0.5 and metrics back to coverage 68%, avg 3.3, gaps 0.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-11\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Saved model edits are not persisted; leaving and reopening the model discards all changes despite a \\\"Model Saved\\\" confirmation\", \"actual\": \"Edited Collaboration to level 5 (coverage rose 68%→75%, avg 3.3→3.7) and clicked Save, which showed the toast \\\"Model Saved — Your changes have been saved successfully.\\\" However nothing was written to storage (localStorage and sessionStorage both empty). After clicking back to the model list and reopening Software Engineer, the model was fully reverted to defaults: collab back to L3 (state prog-lang L4 W0.9, sys-design L3 W0.8, problem-solve L4 W0.9, collab L3 W0.7, code-review L3 W0.6, agile L3 W0.5, all enabled) with coverage 68% and avg 3.3. Since nothing is persisted, a page reload cannot restore the edits either.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"CS-12\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Direct edits are bounded by design: level control offers only buttons 1-5, and the competency weight slider reports aria-valuemin=10 / aria-valuemax=100. Simulation clamping verified: with Technical +2 level / +25% weight, prog-lang went L4→5 (not 6) and W0.9→1.0 (not 1.15), sys-design W0.8→1.0 (not 1.05). With Technical -1 level / -30% weight run repeatedly, technical competencies bottomed out at L1 and W0.1 (10%) and stayed there across further runs (prog-lang, sys-design, code-review all L1 W0.1), never going below 1 or below 10%.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-13\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"All three edit types updated the coverage number and its progress bar in place (URL unchanged, no reload): level change (Programming Languages 4→2) coverage 66%→60%; weight change (slider drag) 68%→67%→66%; enabled change (disable Programming Languages) 66%→65% with bar translateX(-34.857%) = 65.14%; further level change (System Design →5) coverage →75% with bar translateX(-24.545%) = 75.45%. Bar fill tracked the displayed percentage each time.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-19\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"The initially selected Recruitment scenario is not initialized with its default adjustments, so running it without first switching tabs does nothing\", \"actual\": \"On a fresh load of the Software Engineer model, the pre-selected \\\"Recruitment Simulation\\\" tab showed every category adjustment as Level Change 0 / Weight Change 0% (Technical, Behavioral, Leadership, Domain). Clicking Run Simulation without switching tabs left the model completely unchanged: competencies stayed prog-lang L4 W0.9, sys-design L3 W0.8, problem-solve L4 W0.9, collab L3 W0.7, code-review L3 W0.6, agile L3 W0.5 and outputs stayed coverage 68%, avg 3.3, gaps 0. Recruitment's real defaults only appear after navigating to another tab and back (Behavioral +10% weight, Leadership -2 level/-10% weight, Domain -1 level), and only then does running it change outputs.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-20\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Graph node controls never re-render: they display stale level, weight and enabled state, disagree with the summary analysis, and subsequent edits do not start from the current state\", \"actual\": \"After setting Programming Languages to level 2 (summary avg fell 3.3→3.0, gaps 0→1) the node still displayed \\\"Level: Advanced\\\" and showed no Gap indicator even though the summary alert named it as the gap competency. After dragging its weight slider (coverage 68%→67%→66%, matching model weights 0.55 then ~0.15) the node still displayed \\\"Weight 90%\\\" with aria-valuenow=90 and the thumb pinned at x=749 (the 90% position), so the next drag started from the stale 90% position rather than the current value. After disabling it, the toggle still read data-state=\\\"checked\\\", and clicking it again failed to re-enable (coverage remained 65%).\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-15\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"React Flow graph: 6 competency nodes each showing name, description, category, Level and Weight; 5 directed edges (prog-lang→code-review 80%, prog-lang→sys-design 70%, problem-solve→sys-design 90%, collab→code-review 60%, agile→collab 50%) each with arrowclosed markers, visible % weight labels and type-distinguishing stroke colors. Zoom-in button changed scale 0.7358→0.8829; genuine pane drag panned viewport (0,37.7)→(-382,265.7) with node positions unchanged; node drag rearranged layout.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-16\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Critical-path summary and graph highlighting disagree after a weight change; graph highlighting does not recalculate, and the listed path is not a valid path through the edges\", \"actual\": \"Before the change: summary \\\"3 competencies: Programming Languages, Problem Solving, System Design\\\" and 3 nodes highlighted (border-node-critical) — consistent. After lowering Programming Languages' weight 0.9→0.55, the summary immediately became \\\"5 competencies: Problem Solving, System Design, Programming Languages, +2\\\" but the graph still highlighted only the same 3 nodes (prog-lang, sys-design, problem-solve), so summary (5) and highlighting (3) disagree. The listed sequence is also not a valid directed path: edges are prog-lang→sys-design and problem-solve→sys-design, with no edge from Programming Languages to Problem Solving.\", \"evidence\": null}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-17\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Lowering enabled high-weight \\\"Programming Languages\\\" (weight 90%) to level 2 immediately produced gaps=1 and alert \\\"1 competency below recommended level: Programming Languages...\\\". Raising it to level 5 immediately cleared it (gaps=0, alert element removed, coverage 72%, avg 3.5). Re-lowering to level 2 re-created the same named alert, and disabling the competency also cleared it (gaps=0, alert removed).\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-18\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"After lowering Programming Languages to level 2, metrics.gaps.count=1 and a role=\\\"alert\\\" element (metrics.gap-alert) rendered inside the model.metrics region, full-width (1248px), with a lucide-triangle-alert warning icon plus text: \\\"Competency Gap Alert — 1 competency below recommended level: Programming Languages. Consider increasing training focus or adjusting expectations.\\\" — names the affected competency and suggests corrective action.\"}]}"
