    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — **11 PASS, 8 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Working well:** search and filtering (FT-1), model navigation (FT-2), node dragging with correctly re-routed edges (FT-3), the directed weighted graph with pan/zoom/rearrange (CT-15), the template library browse view (FT-6), scenario switching (FT-9), simulation run/reset (FT-10), value clamping (CS-12), live coverage updates (IX-13), and the risk-gap alert lifecycle (CT-17, CT-18).\n\n**Key failures**, most tracing back to one root cause — **the graph node components never re-render after state changes**:\n\n- **IX-20 / FT-5 / FT-7 / CT-16** — nodes keep displaying stale level, weight and enabled values. Disabling a competency correctly changes the math but shows no visual disabled state, and because the toggle is driven by the stale `checked` prop, **a disabled competency can never be re-enabled**. Applying a template updates the title, metrics and adjustments but leaves the graph showing the *previous* model's competencies. Critical-path highlighting likewise doesn't recalculate, so the summary (5 competencies) contradicts the graph (3 highlighted).\n- A related consequence I confirmed by state inspection: **each edit is applied to the original model, discarding prior edits** — setting System Design to L5 then Code Review to L5 silently reverted System Design.\n\n**Genuinely missing features:**\n- **FT-4** — no way at all to edit relationship (edge) weights; no edge handlers exist.\n- **FT-8** — \"Save Current\" is a no-op; no name/description prompt, no *My Templates* collection anywhere.\n- **FT-11** — Save shows \"Model Saved\" but writes nothing to storage; edits are lost on reopening.\n- **IX-19** — the initially selected Recruitment scenario loads with all-zero adjustments, so running it before switching tabs does nothing.\n\nTwo notes on method: node dragging and panning initially appeared broken because Playwright's element-center drags were intercepted by overlapping nodes/edges — both work once targeted correctly. The Radix sliders also ignore keyboard arrow keys, so all slider changes were made via mouse drags."
  }
